## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a practical on-device NLP pipeline for multilingual action-item extraction with privacy, small-footprint models, and edge latency. It also probes labeling strategy and evaluation plans suitable for beginner-level interview candidates.

## Key Concepts
- On-device inference and data locality
- Multilingual action-item extraction with a tiny model
- Lightweight classifier + rule-based fallback
- Privacy controls and deployment considerations
- Evaluation: offline metrics, latency, end-to-end impact

## Code Example
```javascript
// Hint: simple extractor scoring
function scoreItem(item) {
  const keywords = ['asap','urgent','fix','update'];
  let score = keywords.filter(k => item.text.includes(k)).length;
  return Math.min(1, score / keywords.length);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would language-detection errors impact extraction accuracy?
- What are the trade-offs between a tiny Transformer vs a rules-based fallback on edge devices?

## Why This Is Asked

Presents an on-device, multilingual NLP task with concrete constraints (latency, size, privacy) that is accessible to beginners yet touches real-world trade-offs like feature choice, data labeling, and rollout.

## Key Concepts

- On-device inference considerations and WebAssembly deployment
- Lightweight feature engineering (character n-grams) and small classifiers
- Multilingual handling with shared subword spaces
- Privacy-preserving data labeling and audits
- Evaluation: per-language metrics, drift monitoring, staged rollout

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight feature extraction prototype
function extractFeatures(text) {
  const t = text.toLowerCase()
    .replace(/https?:\/\/\S+/g, 'URL')
    .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
    .trim();
  const ngrams = {};
  for (let i = 0; i < t.length - 2; i++) {
    const ng = t.slice(i, i + 3);
    ngrams[ng] = (ngrams[ng] || 0) + 1;
  }
  return ngrams;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale to more languages without inflating size?
- What metrics would you monitor for drift and how would you respond?
- How would you handle ambiguous cases where sarcasm is subtle across cultures?

## Why This Is Asked

Covers on-device NLP, privacy, multilingual support, and practical rollout in fintech contexts. Introduces regulatory-signal detection as a distinct angle from prior questions.

## Key Concepts

- On-device inference vs. cloud; model size planning; rule-based vs. lightweight ML
- Multilingual handling (EN/FR/JA) and term coverage across scripts
- Explainability and confidence calibration for risk flags
- Privacy controls and compliant data labeling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Tiny heuristic-based detector skeleton (pseudo)
class RegRiskDetector {
  constructor(vocab) { this.vocab = vocab; }
  score(text) { return this.vocab.some(t => text.includes(t)) ? 0.9 : 0.1; }
  explain(text) { return this.score(text) > 0.5 ? 'Flag due to regulatory term' : 'No risk'; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evaluate false positives in offline tests?
- What strategies for multilingual term expansion and drift handling would you employ?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine rule-based extraction with lightweight ML under edge constraints in multilingual contexts.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid NLP, on-device inference, multilingual/code-switching, latency, privacy, evaluation.

## Code Example
```javascript
function detectFeatures(text){
  const features = ['battery','screen','camera','price'];
  let mentions = features.filter(f => text.toLowerCase().includes(f));
  return mentions;
}
function scoreSentiment(text){
  const pos = ['great','amazing','love','good','excellent'];
  const neg = ['bad','terrible','worst','poor','fail'];
  let s=0; const tokens = text.toLowerCase().split(/\\W+/);
  tokens.forEach(t => { if(pos.includes(t)) s++; if(neg.includes(t)) s--; });
  return s;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to additional languages and more features?
- How would you handle severe class imbalance or drift over time?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a real-time, privacy-preserving credibility-verification pipeline with multilingual support and explainability, plus practical KVs like drift handling and rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Retrieval-augmented NLP and multilingual inference
- On-device preprocessing and privacy-preserving cloud inference
- Real-time latency budgeting (<250 ms)
- Explainability with citations
- Drift detection, model/versioning, rollback strategy

## Code Example

```javascript
function processMessage(msg) {
  const clean = preprocess(msg);
  const vec = encodeQuery(clean);
  const evidence = retrieveKB(vec);
  const score = scoreCredibility(evidence, msg);
  return explain(evidence, score);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you calibrate the credibility detector to minimize false positives in customer-support contexts?
- How would you design the rollback plan if the KB evidence becomes stale or compromised?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design end-to-end pipeline with privacy, multilingual support, and low latency under real-world incident workloads.

## Key Concepts
- PII redaction, multilingual NER, relation extraction, confidence gating, streaming latency, human-in-the-loop, drift monitoring.

## Code Example
```javascript
// PII redaction sketch
function redact(text) {
  // simple regex for SSN-like patterns
  return text.replace(/[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{4}/g, '[REDACTED_SSN]');
}
```

